.\" Generated from crates/writ-tool/src/specs.rs; do not edit.
.TH WRIT\-GRANT 1 2026-10-12 "writ 0.1.0" "writ manual"
.SH NAME
writ\-grant \- grant TAG to SUBJECT
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nh
.ad l
\fBwrit grant\fR \fB\-i\fR\ \fIDIR\fR [\fB\-\-key\fR\ \fIPATH\fR]... [\fB\-\-delegate\fR] [\fB\-\-expires\fR\ \fIWHEN\fR] [\fB\-\-note\fR\ \fITEXT\fR] [\fB\-q\fR] \fISUBJECT\fR \fITAG\fR
.ad
.hy
.SH DESCRIPTION
Grant TAG to SUBJECT, signed by the identity in DIR.
.PP
A tag is a list of words from general to specific, such as (ssh nas). A grant of (ssh) also covers (ssh nas) and (ssh nas backup). The tag () covers everything.
.PP
With \-\-delegate, SUBJECT can grant TAG, or a narrower tag, to others. A chain of grants can only narrow: each holder gets at most what the grant before it allowed.
.PP
A grant to a name path goes to whoever the path names. For example, a grant to 'me alice laptops' goes to whatever alice calls her laptops, now and in the future.
.PP
SUBJECT can be:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
writ1...                   an id
DIR                        an identity directory (its id is used)
\&'writ1... bob laptop'      a name path: whoever bob calls laptop
FILE                       a public key file, or an OpenSSH .pub file
\&'ssh\-ed25519 AAAA...'      an OpenSSH public key
.fi
.RE
.PP
A grant to a leaf key must expire within 24 hours. writ prints the signed statement and adds it to DIR/issued.writ.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
\fISUBJECT\fR
an id, an identity directory, a key file, or an OpenSSH public key
.TP
\fITAG\fR
a list of words from general to specific, such as '(ssh nas)'
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-identity\fR \fIDIR\fR
the identity that signs the statement
.TP
\fB\-\-key\fR \fIPATH\fR
secret key file (default: DIR/current.key); give one \-\-key per key when several keys must sign; '\-' means standard input
.TP
\fB\-\-delegate\fR
let SUBJECT grant TAG, or part of it, to others
.TP
\fB\-\-expires\fR \fIWHEN\fR
when the statement expires: a duration such as 30d or 12h, or a date such as 2027\-01\-01 (default: never)
.TP
\fB\-\-note\fR \fITEXT\fR
text to show with the statement; writ does not use it
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
print only the statement
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
print help; \fB\-h\fR is short, \fB\-\-help\fR is long
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
success; for questions, the answer is yes
.TP
1
the answer is no: the check, verification, proof or name lookup was denied
.TP
2
an error: bad usage, unreadable input, or a failed operation
.SH EXAMPLES
Let alice log in to nas over SSH:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
writ grant \-i me 'me alice' '(ssh nas)' >> bundle.writ
.fi
.RE
.PP
Let a build machine sign releases for 30 days:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
writ grant \-i me builder '(release)' \-\-expires 30d >> bundle.writ
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBwrit\fR(1), \fBwrit\-name\fR(1), \fBwrit\-revoke\fR(1), \fBwrit\-check\fR(1)
